{
  "concept": "The area under a curve on [a, b] is the limit of sums of inscribed rectangle areas as the number of rectangles increases without bound.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "x-axis with arrowhead, labeled 'x'",
    "y-axis with arrowhead, labeled 'y'",
    "smooth blue curve y = f(x) rising to a local maximum, dipping to a local minimum, then rising again; extends slightly beyond a on the left and beyond b on the right",
    "vertical tick/boundary line at x = a from axis up to the curve, labeled 'a' below the axis",
    "vertical tick/boundary line at x = b from axis up to the curve, labeled 'b' below the axis",
    "light blue shaded exact region between curve and x-axis on [a, b] (panel a state)",
    "set of inscribed rectangles with thin black outlines and light blue fill, heights determined by the sampled function value on each subinterval",
    "unfilled gap slivers between rectangle tops and the curve (visible error region)",
    "panel captions (a), (b), (c) when in side-by-side mode",
    "readout of the number of rectangles n",
    "readout of the Riemann sum approximation value and of the exact area",
    "readout of the error (exact area − Riemann sum)",
    "legend distinguishing 'exact region' shading from 'rectangle approximation'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "Single animated panel"],
      "default": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)"
    },
    {
      "id": "numRectangles",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "As n grows, the inscribed rectangles hug the curve more tightly, the uncovered slivers shrink, and the Riemann sum converges to the exact area.",
      "label": "Number of rectangles n",
      "range": [1, 100, 1],
      "default": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "sampleRule",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Left, right, or midpoint sampling gives different approximations for small n, yet all converge to the same limit — the choice of sample point stops mattering as n → ∞.",
      "label": "Sample point in each subinterval",
      "options": ["Left endpoint", "Midpoint", "Right endpoint"],
      "default": "Left endpoint"
    },
    {
      "id": "refineAnimation",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "Seeing n sweep upward continuously makes the limiting process feel like a single motion: the jagged staircase melts into the smooth shaded region.",
      "label": "Play refinement n → ∞",
      "loop": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The exact area",
      "narration": "Panel (a): the whole region between the curve and the x-axis from a to b is shaded. This is the quantity we want, but we have no formula for a region with a curved top.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "numRectangles": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A coarse approximation",
      "narration": "Panel (b): five inscribed rectangles whose areas we can compute exactly. Their total is close to the true area, but white slivers between each rectangle top and the curve show how much we are missing.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "numRectangles": 5, "sampleRule": "Left endpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Many thin rectangles",
      "narration": "Panel (c): with about twenty-five narrower rectangles the staircase follows the curve closely and the leftover slivers are barely visible. The sum has moved noticeably nearer the exact area.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "numRectangles": 25, "sampleRule": "Left endpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One panel, one motion",
      "narration": "Switch to the single animated panel and drag n yourself. Watch the Riemann sum readout climb toward the exact area while the error readout shrinks toward zero.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "numRectangles": 40, "sampleRule": "Left endpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sample point stops mattering",
      "narration": "At small n, left, midpoint, and right sampling give visibly different sums. Raise n and all three readouts collapse onto the same number — the limit is independent of the sampling rule.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "numRectangles": 8, "sampleRule": "Midpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The limit",
      "narration": "Play the refinement animation: the rectangles thin out until the staircase is indistinguishable from the shaded region. That limiting value is the definite integral of f from a to b.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "numRectangles": 100, "sampleRule": "Left endpoint" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The definite integral is not a new kind of area formula — it is the single number that rectangle sums are forced toward as the rectangles are made arbitrarily thin.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Clean white background, textbook style. In three-panel mode, place three identical axis systems side by side with captions (a), (b), (c) centered below each; in single-panel mode, one large centered axis system. Axes are thin black lines with arrowheads, y-axis labeled 'y' at top, x-axis labeled 'x' at right. The curve is a thick medium-blue (approx #29ABE2) smooth cubic-like shape: it enters low on the left, rises to a rounded maximum roughly one-third across, descends to a shallow minimum about three-quarters across, then rises again at the right edge; it extends slightly past both a and b. Interval endpoints a (left, just right of the y-axis) and b (right) are marked by italic serif labels below the x-axis with thin vertical segments up to the curve. Panel (a) shows the full region under the curve filled with pale blue (#CCE5F0). Panels (b) and (c) show inscribed rectangles with the same pale blue fill and thin black outlines, sitting on the x-axis, tops touching or falling just under the curve — (b) roughly five wide bars, (c) roughly twenty-five narrow bars. Uncovered slivers between bar tops and the curve stay white so the approximation error reads visually. In the single-panel interactive mode, overlay the faint exact shaded region behind the rectangles so the shrinking gap is obvious, and show a compact readout block (n, Riemann sum, exact area, error) in an upper corner. Highlight the error region in a light warm tint when it is large so the learner's eye is drawn to what the rectangles miss."
}

